WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.650
- [Narrator] Thanks to
Indochino for keeping

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.830
LegalEagle in the air

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:05.210
and helping me look fly.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.170
- To this day it's still hard to imagine

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.700
what $2 billion actually looks like

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.868
but I think that jury
wanted to send a message.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.535
(trumpet music)

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.200
- Hey Legal Eagles welcome back

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.510
to the channel and we have a special guest

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:21.970
with us here today.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.380
One of the greatest trial
attorneys in America today

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.380
and an old friend, R. Brent
Wisner with the law firm,

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.490
Baum Hedlund who has I think received

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.960
every accolade you can receive

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.190
as a trial attorney these days

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.050
and I think most people recognize you

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.770
as the attorney who won $2 billion

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.380
from Monsanto just a few weeks ago.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.230
Brent, welcome to the channel.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.063
It's great to have you here.

00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:55.530
- Thank you Devon, it's good to be here.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.040
It's always good to see old friend.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.010
- I think what people would
really like to hear is

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.810
what is it like to win
$2 billion from Monsanto?

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:07.883
- It's pretty unreal,

00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:10.730
I remember sitting in the court room

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.500
when the jury was reading the verdict

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.130
and it was two different plaintiffs,

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.700
both Al and Alberta

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.750
and when Al's verdict came,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.480
he got a billion dollars
in punitive damages

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.030
and I realized, oh my God,

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.765
they're gonna give us two.

00:01:24.765 --> 00:01:26.200
And I remember turned around,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.930
looked at my associate, Pedrim

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.880
and I went (beep)

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.500
and it was pretty unreal.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.189
To this day it's still hard
to imagine what $2 billion

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:36.610
actually looks like

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.140
but I think that jury
wanted to send a message

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.810
and I gave them an opportunity to do that

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.120
and it was pretty cool.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.530
- Let's take a step back for a second.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.160
If people aren't familiar
with the Monsanto litigation.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.150
The Pilliod Case is the
third of three cases

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.823
and you've tried two of them.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.270
Let's start with the first one.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.450
Where you only won $289 million

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.960
that was the Johnson Case.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.310
Can you give us some background about

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.693
what that case was about,

00:02:10.646 --> 00:02:12.630
what the basics of the case were

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.490
and what your theme going
into that trial was?

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.260
- Sure, this case is about
a chemical called glyphosate

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.490
which is a active ingredient
found in the RoundUp herbicide

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.770
or pesticide, just to give you a sense of

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.940
how much has been sprayed in our society.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.100
The amount of glyphosate
that's been sprayed

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.000
in the United States

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.370
by itself is more than every
other pesticide ever combined.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:37.840
It is in everything we eat,

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.980
it is everywhere we go.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.450
And Mr. Johnson and the Pilliods,

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.140
they sprayed RoundUp for a
considerable period of time

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.660
and after spraying it
for a period of time,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.490
they got something called
Non-Hodgkins lymphoma.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.080
Different severities of cancer

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.030
but it is a pretty severe cancer

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.760
and so we won a lawsuit against Monsanto,

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.690
one of the most hated organizations

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.835
or companies in the world and--

00:02:59.835 --> 00:03:00.960
- Allegedly.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.450
- Allegedly, yes, it's really funny.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.873
I've gotten more free drink
at bars telling people

00:03:06.873 --> 00:03:10.200
that I sue Monsanto than
anywhere else in my life.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.660
It's the first thing I say
to somebody when I meet them.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.010
I'm a plaintiffs attorney
and I sued Monsanto

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.283
and they go, let me buy you a drink.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.380
Anyway, run a lawsuit
and I think the theme,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.800
the main principle behind both cases

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.900
in fact it was the first
words out of my mouth

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.440
in both opening statements

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.340
was that this is a case about choice.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.770
It's about the right of every
single person in the courtroom

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.860
to make a choice about what
chemicals they expose themselves

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.140
or their family to

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.180
and knowing whether that it causes cancer

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.490
is part of that choice

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.560
and that Monsanto has robbed that choice

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.460
of every single person in this courtroom,

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.530
the Pilliods, Mr. Johnson and in doing so,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.340
they've caused cancer,
they've actually taken lives

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.420
and so you have to do something to fix it.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.500
That was a big part of the case,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.440
that and also trying to make the case seem

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.120
like a truly historical moment.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.420
That's a really important
way to frame a case.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.030
Jurors don't want to be there

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.430
but if you give them the idea
that they're making history,

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.326
you can really take them
as far as you need to go.

00:04:06.326 --> 00:04:08.061
- There's so much I
want to talk about here.

00:04:08.061 --> 00:04:09.190
- Sure.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.593
- As an attorney myself
and a trial lawyer,

00:04:12.556 --> 00:04:13.920
I'm trumping it a bit to talk

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.950
about every single aspect
of trial tactics here.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.050
Let me start with this.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.930
How did you decide on
that particular theme?

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.830
Is that a theme that carried through both

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:25.870
through the Johnson litigation

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.850
and through the Pillion litigation itself?

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.640
- I spent a lot of time thinking about it

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.130
and I kept coming back to
what is the moment right?

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.273
What is the moment,

00:04:35.318 --> 00:04:38.073
where the actual tort occurs?

00:04:39.513 --> 00:04:41.480
It's not actually when you spray

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.940
because when you're spraying it

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.270
you've already made the decision

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:45.160
to purchase the product.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.830
And I realized that all these
cases come down to a moment

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.210
when you're sitting there in a store,

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.670
a home depot or whatever

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.710
and you're looking at this wall of RoundUp

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:56.960
and you're thinking,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.140
should I buy this stuff to get
rid of the weeds in my house,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.720
that's that moment and
that moment is captured

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.620
by a question of choice

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.720
and I feel like putting the jury

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.140
and putting basically everyone

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.130
in those shoes, gets you
to a place where they go,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.420
okay, I can see how

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:16.840
this lack of disclosure of the cancer risk

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.720
could actually cause a real problem.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.590
That's how we got there

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.050
and it kept going different
ways, different themes

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.530
and I kept coming back to that moment.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.363
- Yeah.

00:05:24.363 --> 00:05:26.480
- And that moment was about choice.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.660
- To take one step back,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.800
this is history making litigation

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:36.800
and you have a long history
of being a trial lawyer

00:05:38.168 --> 00:05:40.470
but my understanding is
that you did not start

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:44.040
with this case, at least
the Johnson litigation

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.613
you were not originally the
trial lawyer for that case.

00:05:48.613 --> 00:05:51.100
How did that come to your desk

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.760
and how did you become
the guy that was going

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.180
to be the lead trial lawyer

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.900
who was going to actually try the thing

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.084
in front of a jury in San Francisco?

00:05:59.084 --> 00:06:02.200
- Devon, you and I know that

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.900
you always wanna be the lead trial lawyer,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:05.242
you always wanna be in
the thick of things.

00:06:05.242 --> 00:06:06.075
- Yeah.

00:06:06.075 --> 00:06:06.908
- You always wanted the opening,

00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:07.741
you always wanted the closing

00:06:07.741 --> 00:06:09.700
because that's what I
live and breathe for.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.500
Back in college when we're doing this

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.680
in our mock trial days at UCLA.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.000
- And we're definitely
gonna talk about that.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.790
- Okay, of course, you did teach

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:17.650
me most of the things I know

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:18.807
about being a trial lawyer of course.

00:06:18.807 --> 00:06:22.188
- Oh stop, flattery
will get you everywhere.

00:06:22.188 --> 00:06:24.690
- Two weeks before the Johnson Trial

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.100
and I'd been working on the
Monsanto RoundUp litigations

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.760
sort of generally for
about a year and a half.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.200
So I knew the science, I knew the experts,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.170
I knew how to prove the
case at least on paper.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.420
Mike Miller who was
the lead trial counsel,

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:40.130
he's seventy years old and
he was out kite surfing

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.000
on Virginia beach.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.080
- Wow.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:42.913
- Power to the guy,

00:06:42.913 --> 00:06:44.500
70 years old and he's still kite-surfing.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.283
Hope I'm doing that when I'm 70.

00:06:47.549 --> 00:06:48.910
And his safety harness didn't work right

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:52.960
and he ultimately went 30
miles per hour into a pier.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.490
He was in the hospital, he almost died.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:55.740
It was pretty horrible.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.970
Well number two in this
firm, Tim Litzenberg,

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:01.260
literally leaves the hospital
and calls me and says,

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.471
hey Brent we got this case
going to trial in two weeks

00:07:03.471 --> 00:07:04.980
can you help out?

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.190
We can't do this by ourselves

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.090
and I said, absolutely
man, whatever you need,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.670
I can do it.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:13.000
The next Monday, he then
had a grand mal seizure

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.840
and he was hospitalized for
seven days and basically I was

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.610
the last man standing
which is one of the reasons

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.620
why I at 35 years old

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.860
was put in this really important position

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.620
of trying a massive historical

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.080
first case against
Monsanto involving RoundUp.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.180
Mike Miller calls me from
his hospital bed and says,

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.100
Brent, go get them and I did.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:38.520
Ironically in the last
trial, in the Pilliod Trial

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.440
in my rebuttal, after the closing

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.170
the last thing I said to the jury

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.820
is I actually looked at them
and I said, go get them.

00:07:45.757 --> 00:07:47.057
Kinda of came full circle.

00:07:48.032 --> 00:07:51.090
- We used to joke in
college and in law school

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:54.510
that the ideal job for a trial lawyer

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.840
is not to have to work all the discovery

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.830
and all the motions and the paperwork

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:02.250
but to just get called
in at the last second

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.320
and get to go to trial.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.623
Somehow you got to a position
where you're living the dream.

00:08:10.741 --> 00:08:14.340
On the one hand sounds
amazing that's exactly

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.880
what we always wanted to do

00:08:17.058 --> 00:08:18.870
but getting a multi-million,

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:22.750
potentially multi-billion
dollar case a week before trial,

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:24.280
that also sounds like
the most terrifying thing

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.170
I've ever heard in my entire life.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:29.090
How in the world do you
go about preparing for

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.480
that kind of a trial

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.540
when the whole world is
gonna be watching you?

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:34.350
- There's a lot of hyperventilating,

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.510
there's a lot of not showing
how scared and nervous you are

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:42.240
but I think also part of being
a trial lawyer is relishing

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:45.780
that pressure, relishing
the anxiety of the moment

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.320
and I actually remember
thinking that morning

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.120
just before the opening statement,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.110
it was like this is it,

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.180
this is what I've been
waiting for my whole life,

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.290
this moment for this trial

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.390
to be brought in complete underdog,

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:03.460
taking on these massive corporate lawyers

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:06.730
who are the most seasoned in the country

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:08.770
and taking them on and
beating the crap out of them

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:10.560
and that's literally the dream

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.370
but one of the things
that people don't realize

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:13.320
when it comes to trying case

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.355
and obviously you know this Devon

00:09:15.355 --> 00:09:18.350
but the trial is this small part.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.480
- Right.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.320
- Of 100's and sometimes
1000's of hours of work

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.160
that leads you to that moment particularly

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.173
in these complex max torque cases.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.690
You got to relish the
moment when you're there.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.500
- Yeah, it's a curse and a blessing

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:38.500
that if you are working on
a case for sometimes years,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.820
you know the case like
the back of your hand,

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.860
it's your baby you know it as well

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.240
as your own physical child

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.700
so when you get to trial,

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.270
you don't have to work it up

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.330
in the same way as if
you were coming in fresh

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:56.330
but without having that
benefit of doing the discovery.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.923
How long was the discovery,

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:01.830
the part of the iceberg that we don't see,

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.060
the trial is the part that we do see.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.830
How long was that litigation going

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.850
on before it even got to trial?

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.165
- To give you some context,

00:10:09.165 --> 00:10:10.250
this is a good way of thinking about it.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.070
There was I think 36 depositions

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.700
that had happened prior to trial.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.110
- That's a good number of depositions.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:18.010
- Each deposition is
seven hours long, minimum

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:20.837
and each of those depositions
probably takes a 100

00:10:20.837 --> 00:10:22.403
to 200 hours of prep.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.920
You start doing the numbers there

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.610
and it becomes sort of astronomical.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.920
In addition to that there was over

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.823
10 million documents produced by Monsanto.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.070
We had scientists from around the world,

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:35.470
we had a lot of third party

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.063
discovery against other scientists.

00:10:39.288 --> 00:10:41.290
It was a complicated case.

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:43.180
One of the things that
we did in this case,

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.207
at least in the Johnson Case
and we did in Pillio as well.

00:10:45.207 --> 00:10:47.670
But in the first case one
of the things that I did

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.540
that everyone thought I was crazy for,

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.230
was I wanted the smartest
jury I could find.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.640
I wanted PhD's, I wanted lawyers,

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.390
I wanted people who could understand

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.950
complex scientific issues on my jury

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:04.140
because I knew that if they
were just lazy not smart people

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.980
or whatever, they wouldn't spend

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:07.160
the time to understand the science

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.300
because when you look at science we win

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.010
but if you just believe the
EPA or whatever, you lose.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.710
You need to have people who are willing

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.350
to look at it truly in a new unit of time

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.320
and give you an actual assessment.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.280
So that was pretty
dangerous trial strategy

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:22.360
but it worked out.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.250
- Yeah, so on the channel

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.330
we talk about the voir dire process

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.322
because that is actually one
of the things that does show up

00:11:28.322 --> 00:11:32.000
in the movies and TV shows
sometimes is that you will see

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.130
the dramatic selection of the jury.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.850
Let's dive into that.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.810
What is your philosophy when
it comes to jury voir dire

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.390
which is the selection of the juries,

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.980
the part that non-lawyers
actually do have some familiarity

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:46.980
with because they get called for jury duty

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.350
and you might be impaneled on the jury

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:53.340
and you might have to get
questioned by an attorney

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:56.270
but how do you go about doing that process

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:58.710
and obviously your philosophy

00:11:58.710 --> 00:11:59.760
with this particular case was

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.270
get the smartest one that you could.

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.620
What does that process look
like from your perspective?

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.490
- It's an interesting
process because most people

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:09.830
who are on that jury, venire

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:12.330
which is the group of people
you're picking a jury from,

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.530
most people don't wanna be there,

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.980
most people want to avoid jury duty.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:19.960
And so the first sort of metric

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.180
is you wanna find the
people who wanna be there

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.580
because people who wanna be
there will be more engaged,

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.820
they'll be more interested.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:26.830
The people who don't wanna be there

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.240
will be half asleep most of the trial.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:30.340
The other thing that you wanna do

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:31.610
is you wanna get them talking.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.720
Lawyers love to hear themselves speak.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.630
It is what we love to do.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.520
- Excuse me, let me vehemently agree

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.800
with your statement there.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:45.840
I'd like to continue to
pontificate and agree

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.174
with that statement about the
vociferousness of attorneys.

00:12:49.174 --> 00:12:51.430
- The loquaciousness of
lawyers is quite astounding

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.270
and how much we like to talk about things

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:54.790
that we even agree on at times.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.233
- That, a 100% agreed.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:12:59.560
- One thing is to get them talking

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:00.730
because you need a jury to talk.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.770
You need them to say
things and speak their mind

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:04.787
but I think one of the other things

00:13:04.787 --> 00:13:06.310
and this is a strategy

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.940
that we use particularly in San Francisco.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.530
You're in San Francisco,
this is not a jury pool

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.820
that likes Monsanto, this is not a jury

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.060
pool that likes GMO's.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.470
These are people who eat organic

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:21.050
and want to live a healthier lifestyle.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:24.410
There's a natural dislike
of Monsanto walking in.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.100
One of the things that we
did from the very beginning

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.810
is to downplay our case.

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.400
The first thing we say to the jury is,

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.510
nothing can be proven,
cancer can't be proven

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:36.510
that's impossible, who here agrees?

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:37.530
Who here agrees?

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:38.720
Because we want people to say,

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:39.780
yeah, you can't prove cancer.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.820
We want them to sort of dislike the case

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:42.820
because when they actually get

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.520
into the jury trial we're gonna win them.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.485
But we wanna weed out the bad people.

00:13:46.485 --> 00:13:49.710
So much of our job is
finding the bad jurors

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:53.920
and then trying to get them to
be willing to be on the jury

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.210
'cause it's so easy to
get out of jury duty,

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.430
you just say, oh I hate
Monsanto, I can't be impartial

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:58.263
and then you're off.

00:13:58.263 --> 00:14:00.330
You got to get those
people to not say that

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.480
or say, oh I don't like Monsanto

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:04.150
but I can put that aside and be impartial.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.350
That was the fight when it
comes to jury selection,

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:08.550
that is the fight.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.130
- Yeah, so let's dig down
into some of the minutia.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:14.720
When I was trying a case,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:18.535
I wanted to be as relatable
as possible to the jury

00:14:18.535 --> 00:14:20.920
and you're not allowed to
talk to the jury outside

00:14:21.858 --> 00:14:25.533
of the selection process
and the actual trial itself

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:28.580
but I remember I was walking
through a metal detector

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:31.130
and I saw some of the
members of the potential jury

00:14:32.967 --> 00:14:35.470
and I made a big show of
taking the metal pieces

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:36.380
out of my pocket like,

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.710
ah gosh, I'm stuck here just like you are.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:44.620
Is there anything that
you do to use every piece

00:14:45.875 --> 00:14:47.950
of the court process available to you

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.070
so that you can ingratiate yourself

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.730
with the jury and the judge

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:54.816
and sometimes even the
bailiff and people like that?

00:14:54.816 --> 00:14:59.178
- The first thing you do
is you introduce everybody.

00:14:59.178 --> 00:15:01.693
Not just the lawyers, the paralegal,

00:15:02.653 --> 00:15:04.810
the person who's operating the tech,

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:07.110
at my last trial I actually
introduced my parents

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:08.940
who were in the gallery.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:12.300
I introduced Bobby Kennedy
who had been a mentor of mine,

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:15.580
it was like a name parade
of all these people

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.950
who were part of our team.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:20.130
It shows that you care
about everybody on your team

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.400
and I think people related
to that immediately.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:23.510
The other thing that you have to do

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.850
is not be above anything.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.230
So many lawyers are like,
I'm not gonna move chairs,

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.200
I'm not gonna deal with
binders or whatever.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:31.820
I have people for that.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.640
I never let anybody do anything but me.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:35.860
I'm the one moving chairs,

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.340
I'm the one handing the mic around

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:39.020
giving it to different jurors.

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:42.710
They see that I'm a person
who has no sense of pride

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:45.940
or ego that I'm just there to do my job

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:48.940
and I think that builds an
incredible amount of credibility.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:52.190
Actually at the Pilliod Case it was so bad

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:55.200
that during voir dire there was a break,

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.620
the judge actually go,
the defense council goes,

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.480
judge, Mr. Wisner is being too helpful,

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.690
he's handing around microphones
and glad-handing the jury,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:03.792
can you tell him to stop doing that?

00:16:03.792 --> 00:16:04.625
- Oh my God.

00:16:04.625 --> 00:16:05.458
- It was actually quite funny.

00:16:05.458 --> 00:16:08.210
- Is there anything you can
do in a jury Voir dire process

00:16:10.035 --> 00:16:14.850
to show that you are just
one of everybody else

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:18.000
apart from taking all the boxes

00:16:18.913 --> 00:16:20.670
and drawing an objection

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:21.950
that you're being too helpful?

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:24.013
That's a fantastic objection to get.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.240
- I think it's really important to relate.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:31.020
First of all, put yourself
in the shoes of those jurors.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:33.970
They don't wanna be
there, they wanna do jobs

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:35.510
or worry about how long
the trial's gonna be

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:36.343
they're worried about,

00:16:36.343 --> 00:16:38.143
oh my God am I not gonna
get paid for six weeks.

00:16:38.143 --> 00:16:40.629
There's so many things
that they're worried about.

00:16:40.629 --> 00:16:42.150
One of the things is

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:43.750
everyone starts introducing
themselves at the very beginning

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:44.680
hi my name is blah-di-blah,

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:45.770
hey my name is blah-di-blah.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:46.850
I always go up there and go,

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:50.510
hey, hey everyone and I
get them to say hey back

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:51.770
and I go, I'm so sorry.

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.400
I know none of you wanna be here,

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:54.900
frankly it sucks

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.570
but I'm gonna do everything
I can to get you out of here

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.667
but if I pick ya, it's because
I really like ya, sorry.

00:17:00.667 --> 00:17:02.214
And they all start laughing.

00:17:02.214 --> 00:17:05.730
And you immediately start
creating a rapport with them

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:09.330
in a sort of genuine way not
in like a false pretense way.

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.650
For example the defense lawyer

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:12.483
he actually saw me doing that.

00:17:12.483 --> 00:17:14.240
and he was like ah I gotta
do something charming too.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:15.090
He goes up there and he goes,

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:19.200
hey everyone, I'm from Nevada, Las Vegas

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.713
but don't hold it against
me that the Raiders left.

00:17:23.757 --> 00:17:24.807
And everyone's like--

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:27.300
- Yeah, San Francisco
is not the right town

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:28.135
to make that joke.

00:17:28.135 --> 00:17:31.374
- Exactly, he didn't
understand where he was.

00:17:31.374 --> 00:17:33.880
That's assuming a lot of things.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:35.240
If he'd made a Warriors reference

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.360
that would have been a
really good thing to do.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.894
But he didn't get it,

00:17:38.894 --> 00:17:40.397
sort of relating to them in a real way

00:17:40.397 --> 00:17:44.550
and I think a lack of
sincerity is obvious.

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.730
When someone's been
untruthful, you know it

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.640
but when someone's being
real you mean like,

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:49.473
oh, this guy's a real person.

00:17:49.473 --> 00:17:52.210
I like him and that's a
really important part.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.030
You don't win the case
by the jury liking you.

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:56.620
Jury can hate you and
you can still win or lose

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.000
but if they like you,

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.890
it takes away a weapon of the defendant

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:02.810
because when they attack
you in closing argument

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.060
or they attack you in front of the jury

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:06.690
the jury gets defensive.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.000
They go, what are you
doing, I like that guy.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:09.510
What are you doing, you can't attack him.

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:10.820
That's actually happened
in all the trials.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:13.010
Monsanto always comes after me personally

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.610
and it always backfires,
every single time.

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:19.180
- Yeah, that's so huge
because a lot of lawyers

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:20.820
and I probably put myself in this camp

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:24.963
say that the trial itself is
won and lost on credibility.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:30.130
You have one opportunity to put your case

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:32.830
out there and if the jury doesn't like you

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.300
or the judge doesn't like you
it's gonna be a huge uphill

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.170
battle and maybe credibility
won't win the case for you

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:42.083
but if you lose your credibility
I think you're just done.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:45.380
There's no way to recover from that.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.510
Are there things that happened

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:49.230
in the Johnson or Pilliod
case that you think

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.190
bolstered your credibility
and the credibility

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:53.543
of your witnesses

00:18:53.543 --> 00:18:56.020
that helped build that in
front of the judge and jury?

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:59.500
- Yeah, there's a split within
the trial lawyer community.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.200
There's the group that
says never show weakness.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.530
Always be right, always be the
alpha dog in the courtroom.

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:07.100
I don't agree with that at all,

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:10.400
I think that's the terrible
approach particularly

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.220
in a more dynamic and diverse

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:14.443
environment like San Francisco.

00:19:14.443 --> 00:19:16.050
I think being real is way more important

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:17.650
and so for example in the Pilliod case

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:18.750
during my opening statement

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:21.420
I actually said a fact that was wrong.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:25.070
I said that they had sprayed
over 1500 gallons of RoundUp

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:27.930
and it's actually they'd
sprayed it for 1500 days

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.780
but not 1500 gallons

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.300
and I said that wrong

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:32.930
and I realized that I had said that wrong

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.100
and so the first witness

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.440
I had that had to deal
with this issue, I go,

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:41.420
doctor Sawyer, listen
during opening statement

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:44.989
I told this jury that
they'd sprayed 1500 gallons,

00:19:44.989 --> 00:19:46.610
is that true?

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:48.670
No it's not, what did I get wrong?

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:51.330
Well, it's 1500 days,
thank you doctor Sawyer.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.200
I apologize ladies and gentlemen
I shouldn't have done that.

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:55.670
I just say it, I own it

00:19:55.670 --> 00:20:00.270
and you do that and you
chose a level, a willingness

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.090
to be wrong in front of a jury
I think it goes a long way

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:04.800
because they know I'm
not trying to fool them

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.910
that I care more about the truth

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:09.850
than I do necessarily about being right

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:11.840
and if you do that

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.000
then it leads them to the world of

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.900
okay, here's the evidence
I believe that evidence.

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:18.864
Here's their evidence I see that evidence,

00:20:18.864 --> 00:20:20.317
I'm going with the guy I believe.

00:20:20.317 --> 00:20:21.820
And that's how you win.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:23.150
- God, that's such a great point

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:27.350
because no case is ever gonna be perfect.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:28.690
You're going to have bad facts

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:30.070
or you're gonna have bad law

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:32.960
and you're going to
have to craft your case

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:34.440
around those

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:37.160
potential weaknesses and I love that story

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.700
because you're turning a weakness

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.140
into a strength.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:44.470
You made a misstatement
in your opening statement

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:47.350
and if it just stayed there

00:20:47.350 --> 00:20:49.290
the other side is going
to have a transcript,

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:51.162
they're gonna have the recordings

00:20:51.162 --> 00:20:52.760
and closing argument
they're gonna bring that out

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:55.340
and just say you can't trust Mr. Wisner

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:56.690
because he made this statement,

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:57.523
- Exactly.

00:20:57.523 --> 00:21:00.810
- And instead you used that
as part of your case in chief

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:03.000
you come out, you acknowledge the mistake

00:21:04.177 --> 00:21:05.010
and in the grand scheme of things

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:08.080
it doesn't matter, it's not
like the jury is going to

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:12.010
rely on this one fact forever

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:16.630
but now you have shown that
you are a trustworthy person.

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:17.463
- Exactly.

00:21:17.463 --> 00:21:21.120
- And even if it makes you
look stupid in the short-term

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.490
that you did something wrong
or you had a misstatement

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:26.670
or you made a flub,

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:28.670
the jury knowing that they can trust you

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.300
is probably more important
at the end of the day.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.133
- And you know where you see

00:21:32.133 --> 00:21:35.160
that the most is actually
in direct examination.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.330
Direct examinations are a show.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.910
You have your witness, you
know what they're gonna say.

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:41.480
You've practiced the questions,

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:44.260
you know what their opinions are,

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:47.230
there's no surprise here but what I'll do,

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:50.380
is I'll actually ask some
pretty harsh questions

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:52.180
to my own witness, I'll go,

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:55.200
hold on doctor, wait a second

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.676
who are you to say the EPA is wrong?

00:21:57.676 --> 00:21:59.950
What gives you the right to say

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:01.270
that this organization is wrong?

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:02.930
And obviously, I've cleared this

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:04.880
question with them beforehand.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:06.370
I'm not just dropping this in their lap.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:10.280
I play a sort of devil's
advocate in my direct

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:12.380
and it creates so much credibility

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:13.640
because there's somebody in that jury

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:15.180
who had that exact question

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.555
who's like wait a second,
who's this person?

00:22:17.555 --> 00:22:18.530
And you always have a great answer

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:20.780
for example doctor Portier he was saying

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:23.130
how the EPA didn't apply
their own guidelines

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:24.380
to glyphosate and that's why

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.280
they came to a different conclusion

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.060
and I'm like, hold on doctor Portier.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:28.893
Who are you?

00:22:28.893 --> 00:22:30.480
Who are you to tell this jury

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.750
that they're not following
the EPA guidelines?

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:33.792
- This is your own witness.

00:22:33.792 --> 00:22:34.625
- This is my own witness.

00:22:34.625 --> 00:22:35.458
I'm like, come on.

00:22:35.458 --> 00:22:37.073
Who's this guy think he is?

00:22:37.073 --> 00:22:40.293
Then he goes, well Mr. Wisner
I wrote the guidelines.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:43.850
Okay, then I move on

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:45.660
but that's a more
powerful way of getting to

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:47.950
that question and answer that saying,

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:48.840
so you wrote the guidelines,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:49.939
does that mean you can criticize,

00:22:49.939 --> 00:22:51.120
you know what I mean?

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.710
Being a sort of
confrontational direct examiner

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:55.850
can go a long way to sort of convincing

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.300
your jury that you
really do have the guts.

00:22:58.300 --> 00:22:59.960
- I think that's a great segue

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:03.610
into some of the
inaccuracies of what people

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:06.060
see on TV and in movies.

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:08.220
Obviously nobody's gonna wanna watch

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.980
a movie that deals with literally

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.430
years of just reading
documents and writing briefs.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:16.880
It's no surprise that
Hollywood will try and condense

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:19.970
these things into a more palatable drama

00:23:19.970 --> 00:23:22.080
although you can come to LegalEagle

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:24.780
to watch me talk about the accuracy

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:27.090
or the lack there of in all
these TV shows and movies

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:31.440
but given that, the trial
itself can last days

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:33.630
or weeks and sometimes months for example

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.770
how long was Johnson
and how long was Pilliod

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:37.043
- The Johnson case was six weeks

00:23:37.043 --> 00:23:39.060
and the Pilliod case was nine weeks.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:42.770
- Okay, wow imagine being on
the jury for nine weeks having

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:46.130
to be away from your family and your job.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:47.320
Obviously a lot of this is going

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:50.970
to be shuffling papers,
entering things into evidence.

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:54.010
How do you keep the jury's attention

00:23:55.353 --> 00:23:56.830
and make sure that they pay attention

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.860
and get the really important facts

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:00.810
even when you're dealing with sometimes

00:24:01.781 --> 00:24:05.473
really elaborate and
obscure scientific issues?

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:08.183
- It's really interesting,

00:24:08.183 --> 00:24:10.210
I'd love to see how cases were tried

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:12.410
before the film industry

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.747
because it's really a
chicken and egg situation

00:24:15.747 --> 00:24:18.340
because everyone has seen so many movies

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.450
where they see all this drama,

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:21.710
they've seen "Law And Order"

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.117
that's actually why I became a
lawyer I like "Law And Order"

00:24:23.117 --> 00:24:24.917
bet that's something that'll be fun.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:31.010
My job in many ways is to
create sort of cinematic drama

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:34.320
in the courtroom when it
wouldn't naturally be there

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.720
and so there's different
ways of doing that.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.070
I try like so when I'm cross examining

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:41.780
I make it a point to storm
around the courtroom,

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:44.140
throw documents, slam them on the ding,

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:46.580
point, cajole and I do that

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.160
not necessarily because
it's more convincing

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:50.910
but because the jury goes form like

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:54.320
ah, oh what's gonna happen next?

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:56.500
They get into I'm watching a movie now

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:57.799
this is what I thought trials were about.

00:24:57.799 --> 00:24:59.490
- Right, right.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.470
- It's funny, Hollywood has
actually created an onus on us

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:05.693
to make it Hollywood when it
wouldn't naturally be that way.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:07.970
It comes in different
way and I think that,

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.380
it comes in cross examination
'cause there's tension there.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:11.213
- Yeah.

00:25:11.213 --> 00:25:12.800
- It comes in openings,
it comes in closings.

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.400
There's a really powerful thing
to do when you're on direct

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.110
and it's something that very
few attorneys are willing to do

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:20.030
again it goes to credibility

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:23.400
but it's where I ask
the witness a question

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:24.630
the answer is bad for me

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:26.343
and I'll be like,

00:25:26.343 --> 00:25:29.120
so sir, based on all this
it clearly causes cancer

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:31.850
and he's like, no I wouldn't say that.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:33.900
And the jury looks at me like, oh my God.

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.090
What just happened?

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:36.747
This has all gone sideways there

00:25:36.747 --> 00:25:38.010
and it makes it entertaining.

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:40.870
You have to create those
dramas as much as you can

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:43.040
and I'll be honest I did some
of that in the Pilliod case

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:46.713
I think in a way, I
probably angered the judge.

00:25:47.748 --> 00:25:48.581
Did I tell you--

00:25:48.581 --> 00:25:49.827
- Yeah, do tell.

00:25:49.827 --> 00:25:52.570
So you crossed a line
and made too much drama?

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:53.820
- I took it a little far.

00:25:54.802 --> 00:25:56.780
It's a RoundUp case, it's pesticide

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:59.050
and we actually had a
container of the RoundUp

00:25:59.050 --> 00:26:01.040
that Mr. and Mrs. Pilliod actually used

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.670
on their properties and
we had cleaned it out.

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:06.620
It was totally cleaned out
and I had expert on there

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:07.710
and I pick it up and he's like,

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:09.140
you should be wearing gloves and I'm like,

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:11.990
oh, sure, put on gloves and I'm holding it

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:13.030
and that wasn't planned

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:16.160
that actually was a natural
sort of thing that occurred.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:19.070
And as I'm kind of
demonstrating how it gets used

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.620
and it shows how it gets on
your leg and all that stuff,

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:24.410
I realized that there was
a hydraulic pump on it

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:25.243
and I was like,

00:26:25.243 --> 00:26:26.200
ah, look at that, it's still working.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.017
And so I pulled the
hydraulic pump and I said,

00:26:28.017 --> 00:26:29.610
you go like this and you spray it.

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:31.690
And this mist came out.

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:33.998
And it was right next to the jury

00:26:33.998 --> 00:26:34.964
and two of the jurors were like

00:26:34.964 --> 00:26:35.797
(shrieking)

00:26:35.797 --> 00:26:38.220
one juror was like wiping
his face all freaked out.

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:40.730
And I was like, oh boy

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:41.563
and the judge is like,

00:26:41.563 --> 00:26:43.550
Mr. Wisner, why don't you
put that away right now.

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:45.820
I was like, yes judge, I put it away.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:47.630
And then one of the
jurors wrote in a question

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.980
'cause they can write questions
in California State Court

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:52.030
and they said, what was in the container?

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:56.720
And we had this long debate
with the counsel and the judge

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.300
and the judge ultimately
gave a curative instruction

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:01.200
which is disregard or whatever

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:03.560
and the original curative
instruction that Monsanto wanted

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:05.790
was to disregard everything
I said about the bottle.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:08.020
I was like, your honor
that's not really fair.

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:09.470
There was lot of relevant testimony,

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:10.980
shouldn't really just be
about what was in the bottle.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:12.250
She's like, okay fine.

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:13.700
The jury comes back in and she goes,

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:16.690
ladies and gentlemen I
just want you to know

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:19.360
that there was only water in that bottle

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.116
so it's perfectly safe.

00:27:21.116 --> 00:27:24.000
I probably pushed that a little too far.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:26.780
When I have a juror who's
worried about being sprayed

00:27:26.780 --> 00:27:29.010
with chemicals I've probably
taken the drama too far

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:30.170
but they paid attention.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:32.170
- I mean that's an advance trial tactic.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.090
I love that sort of inside baseball

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:37.250
that actual trial tactics

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:40.330
have been changed because
of the public perception

00:27:40.330 --> 00:27:43.460
and again as any good
trial lawyer would do,

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.450
you're turning potential disadvantage

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:50.450
that people are bored from
watching a multi-day, multi-week

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:53.144
trial and you're turning that on it's head

00:27:53.144 --> 00:27:55.750
by creating drama to punctuate

00:27:57.708 --> 00:27:59.608
the points that you're trying to make.

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:01.840
In these particular cases

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:06.640
do you think that other
attorneys are using the same

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:08.870
opportunities that you are or do you think

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:11.110
that you are outside the norm

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:13.070
in being able to create that drama

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:16.020
and use that emotional
punch when you need to?

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.170
- I think that there's
some plaintiff lawyers,

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:19.530
trial lawyers who are doing this

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:21.880
on a regular basis very effectively.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:23.970
In fact I've learned
a lot of my techniques

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:26.070
other than of course from you

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:28.850
from when we were in college
and doing mock trial at UCLA

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:31.540
but Mark Lanier he's probably considered

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:33.370
the most preeminent trial lawyer

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:35.620
in the world plaintiff side,

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:37.970
and I've learned a lot of
these techniques from them.

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:40.720
How to create interest
where other one's lacking.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:44.270
A really good example is depositions.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:45.785
We take these seven hours depositions.

00:28:45.785 --> 00:28:46.618
- Right.

00:28:46.618 --> 00:28:49.880
- You cannot play a seven
hour deposition to a jury.

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:51.433
They will kill you, okay.

00:28:51.433 --> 00:28:52.870
They're so boring

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.750
and there's so many legal,
technical questions I get asked.

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:58.100
- The deposition being questions taking

00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:00.933
under oath outside of the trail itself.

00:29:00.933 --> 00:29:02.700
It's part of the discovery process

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:04.280
where you're in a conference room.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:05.610
You're just asking questions,

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:07.960
there's a stenographer
writing down the answers

00:29:09.074 --> 00:29:10.100
and sometimes things go wrong

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:11.090
and you might have to call the judge

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:12.740
but for the most part
the judge is not there.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.743
It's just you, the witness
and the posing counsel.

00:29:17.652 --> 00:29:19.070
Now they're limited to seven hours,

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:21.140
before you could go much longer.

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:23.740
The federal rules have
allowed you to go longer now.

00:29:24.946 --> 00:29:28.140
Yeah, these can be very,
very boring things.

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:29.970
- Hey Legal Eagles, it's
Devon from the future.

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:31.380
I just wanted to pop in real quick

00:29:31.380 --> 00:29:33.220
and let you know that
today's interview was brought

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.620
to you by Indochino my
favorite suit maker.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:37.630
I'm wearing an Indochino suit right now.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:40.380
I was wearing an Indochino
suite in the past

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:42.020
when I was conducting this interview.

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:43.060
It actually worked out really well.

