back to the interview with a guy

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:34.337
that's gonna take down Monsanto.

00:30:34.337 --> 00:30:35.757
- You know one of the things--

00:30:35.757 --> 00:30:39.090
And obviously you wanna
play certain portions of it.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:42.580
In California because of the
subpoena limitation powers,

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:45.240
I can't force a Monsanto witness

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:49.025
in St. Louis to come testify
at my trial, I can't.

00:30:49.025 --> 00:30:52.260
I only can play videos from deposition.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:55.460
One of the things that we do
to make them more interesting

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:57.300
is we use three cameras.

00:30:57.300 --> 00:31:01.030
We use one on the questioner,
one on the witness,

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:04.700
we have a document camera or a whiteboard.

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:07.470
We even have a camera on like
documents in another way,

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:09.100
we can actually afford cameras

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.540
and what we do is we film
all of them at the same time

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:12.970
and we play it with the jury.

00:31:12.970 --> 00:31:14.410
It's like a television show.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:15.243
It goes to the question,

00:31:15.243 --> 00:31:16.270
it goes to the answer,
it goes to the question,

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:17.103
it goes to the answer

00:31:17.103 --> 00:31:18.970
because they've done studies.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.410
People are used to watching
clips for 12 seconds

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:22.490
at a time.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:23.323
- Right, right.

00:31:23.323 --> 00:31:24.590
- A screen changes every 12 seconds

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:26.330
and if you don't have that, it gets boring

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:28.000
and they fall asleep.

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:31.430
We've had a lot of really good reactions

00:31:31.430 --> 00:31:34.260
causing that back and
worth in our depositions

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:35.720
and they kinda become kind of more

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:36.980
dramatic and kind of more fun

00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:38.990
and it goes to the document camera

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:39.823
and it goes to the person

00:31:39.823 --> 00:31:43.360
and one of them actually
did where I was standing

00:31:43.360 --> 00:31:44.890
and the camera was following me

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:46.960
as I was asking questions like full on.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:48.250
- You were standing in a deposition?

00:31:48.250 --> 00:31:49.710
- Yeah, they were filming me

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.170
as I was walked around

00:31:51.170 --> 00:31:53.090
so it could look more authentic,

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:54.695
become more interesting for the jury.

00:31:54.695 --> 00:31:56.171
- Wow, three cameras.

00:31:56.171 --> 00:31:57.700
Can you imagine someone being

00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:59.613
so vain as to use three cameras.

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:02.240
- shocking, you would know.

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:03.750
- Yeah.

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:04.670
But I've actually never heard of that.

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:07.340
That's a great tactic to know

00:32:08.397 --> 00:32:10.350
that some depositions are
gonna be important enough

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.120
that you're going to show it to the jury

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:17.120
in trial so you use a multi-cam
setup to be able to cut

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:19.580
while they're saying the same thing,

00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:21.430
it's not breaking up the actual testimony

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:25.570
but you're using it to keep
the attention of the jury,

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:28.330
I mean you're thinking
so many steps ahead.

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:29.730
- Whenever you're litigating,

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:31.720
obviously different lawyers
have different approaches

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:34.370
but at least within my firm
and at least personally,

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:36.760
everything is towards the trial.

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:40.130
Every question I ask is how is
this gonna be used at trial.

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:41.350
Everything has to be.

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:43.770
And if you do that and you do it right

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:45.610
then you get to go to trial
and get $2 billion verdicts.

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:49.910
That's how you do it and
so many lawyers are so busy

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:51.330
so used to settling or so used

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:53.690
to not actually being a trial lawyer.

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:54.600
That's the big difference

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:55.433
that you see today

00:32:55.433 --> 00:32:58.510
is that good trial lawyers
have everything about trial

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:00.490
and how we're gonna present it.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:01.570
We do things,

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:05.690
I'm pretty maniacal for
example like my table,

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:07.240
a counselor table in front

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.290
of a jury is meticulously organized.

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:11.440
I have everything in the same spot

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:13.290
because studies have shown that if you

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:16.240
look more organized,
juries believe you more.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:17.990
Even you said nothing different except

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:20.150
that you have an
organized desk, that's it.

00:33:20.150 --> 00:33:21.950
And it's amazing how little things

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:24.500
like that can really cue
a jury in to being like,

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:26.120
okay this guy is for real, I trust him.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:26.953
- Yeah.

00:33:26.953 --> 00:33:28.440
- It's hard because I'm so young,

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:30.280
I don't have that much gray hair,

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:33.040
I don't have the seasoned old person thing

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:35.510
so I got to show that I know everything.

00:33:35.510 --> 00:33:37.270
Everyone in that jury has to believe

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:39.080
that lawyer knows more than everybody

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:41.593
and everyone believes him
and that's kind my goal.

00:33:41.593 --> 00:33:42.770
- That's so funny because

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:44.630
- Do you remember in mock trial when

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:46.410
we had the idea that we would get

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:48.830
rid of all the paperwork
on the desk at all.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:52.490
And only have this like
this tiny 8 x 5 little

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:53.851
sheet to scribble notes on it.

00:33:53.851 --> 00:33:57.130
That would be the only thing
we had at counsel table.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:33:58.340
I don't think that worked very well.

00:33:58.340 --> 00:34:00.450
You must have improved on that.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:01.860
- We have similar systems but,

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:04.230
I'll tell you one thing
that we haven't changed,

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:05.563
is I don't use a script.

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:09.030
Every judge always comments,

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:10.520
Mr. Wisner, you don't have any notes

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:11.410
when you're cross examining.

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:12.400
And I go absolutely not.

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:14.790
And that's something I
learned at UCLA at mock trial.

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:16.960
And it blows the jury away.

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:19.160
I mean, blows them away cause they're

00:34:19.160 --> 00:34:20.500
this guy does it all in his head.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:21.645
This guys knows everything.

00:34:21.645 --> 00:34:22.560
- It's like what they see on TV.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:23.560
- Exactly.

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:24.550
Now here is one trick,

00:34:24.550 --> 00:34:25.890
a technological trick.

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:27.090
So when I'm doing my closing,

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:28.720
I always use a PowerPoint, right?

00:34:28.720 --> 00:34:30.660
But what I do is I actually take a screen,

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:32.620
I put it at the base of the

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:37.420
where the bar is, between
the jury and the lawyers,

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:40.940
and I have the next
slide that's gonna be up,

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:43.580
so, I know what the
next slide is gonna be,

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:44.970
as I'm doing it.

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:46.880
They don't know that I
know it's the next slide.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:47.896
So I'm if I'm going through,

00:34:47.896 --> 00:34:50.450
and I'm like you know what,
that is exactly what he said.

00:34:50.450 --> 00:34:51.540
Boom, and all of the sudden it comes out

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:52.373
and they're like,

00:34:52.373 --> 00:34:53.390
how does this guy know?

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:54.570
He knows everything.

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:56.120
It's just like he sees into the future

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:58.240
and it's just cause it's right
there in front of my feet.

00:34:58.240 --> 00:34:59.140
- That's awesome.

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:01.062
What do you think are the most

00:35:01.062 --> 00:35:03.730
pivotal parts of the trial, itself?

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:05.550
Because a lot of it is monotonous.

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.010
A lot of it is perfunctory.

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:08.940
And some of it is duplicative.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:12.080
What do you think are the
parts of the trial itself

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:15.890
where the trial is won or
lost in front of the jury?

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.580
- I mean I think there is
really a couple of clear moments

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:21.030
where it's make or break in the case.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.150
The first is Voir dire, jury selection,

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:23.983
there's no question.

00:35:23.983 --> 00:35:26.400
I mean, you picked the wrong
jury you're gonna lose.

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:28.310
You pick the right jury you can win,

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:31.200
and you can lose a case
with voir dire very easily.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:33.590
I remember one, my first
case I ever tried actually

00:35:33.590 --> 00:35:34.423
Alexander in Virginia

00:35:34.423 --> 00:35:35.256
you actually came and saw it.

00:35:35.256 --> 00:35:36.230
- I came to your closing.

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:37.870
- You came to my closing.

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:39.445
I did the opening
statement and Mike Miller

00:35:39.445 --> 00:35:42.560
the same guy who actually
tried the Pilliod case with me,

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:46.060
and the same guy who was
injured for the Johnson case,

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:48.670
he actually came to my opening
statement in that trial.

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:50.960
He went in, he saw the opening statement,

00:35:50.960 --> 00:35:53.080
comes back to the war
room and he goes Brent,

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:54.760
I just want you to know that
was probably one of the best

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:57.060
opening statements I've
ever seen in my life.

00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:58.583
And you're gonna lose.

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:01.300
I said, what do you mean?

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:03.150
He goes, look at your jury.

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:05.430
And I looked at my jury
and there's nothing

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:06.427
I can say to win that jury.

00:36:06.427 --> 00:36:07.990
And it was never gonna happen.

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:09.560
It was not gonna, it was never

00:36:09.560 --> 00:36:10.900
I was never gonna win that trial.

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:13.510
So you can make or break
the case in Voir dire.

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:15.110
The next moment is opening statement.

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:15.943
- Okay,

00:36:15.943 --> 00:36:17.730
- Because that's the first time
you're talking substantively

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:18.563
about the case.

00:36:18.563 --> 00:36:20.290
And the jury is listening.

00:36:20.290 --> 00:36:21.820
And some people have
different theories about it.

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:22.870
Some people say well,

00:36:24.571 --> 00:36:25.430
it has to be short.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:26.263
Punchy.

00:36:26.263 --> 00:36:27.096
Get in and out.

00:36:27.096 --> 00:36:28.440
I don't agree with that at all.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:29.730
If you're gonna make a jury

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:31.340
give you billions of dollars

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:33.020
you have to show them that you earned it.

00:36:33.020 --> 00:36:34.430
And you gotta show them everything.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:36.150
My opening statements run
between three and a half

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:37.070
to four hours.

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:38.330
They're very long.

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:40.690
And then the next moment
is your first witness.

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:41.523
Right?

00:36:41.523 --> 00:36:42.640
Because either they're
gonna listen to what

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:43.770
what do you have to say,

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:45.340
let's hear what your
witnesses have to say.

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:48.100
You always bring your rock
star as your first witness.

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:50.510
No question and that one
should be the mos polished

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:51.560
the most clean.

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:53.360
There should be no real
evidentiary issues,

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:55.860
that's the one where you just hammer them.

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:57.980
And the last and probably
the most important part,

00:36:57.980 --> 00:36:59.570
very often people think
it's closing argument

00:36:59.570 --> 00:37:01.120
but it's actually not.

00:37:01.120 --> 00:37:02.790
By the time you get to closing argument

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:04.793
the jury's heard openings,
they've heard all the testimony,

00:37:04.793 --> 00:37:05.626
- Right, right.

00:37:05.626 --> 00:37:06.830
- I think they know what they're gonna go.

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:07.663
Where they're going.

00:37:07.663 --> 00:37:09.410
They know what they think.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:10.950
It's their first witness.

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:13.170
So, the defendant's first witness.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:15.280
And there is no question that

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:18.590
that moment you have to obliterate them.

00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:22.567
You have to destroy that witness as hard

00:37:22.567 --> 00:37:24.160
and as aggressively as you can

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.300
because if you can take
out their first witness

00:37:26.300 --> 00:37:27.750
they are done.

00:37:27.750 --> 00:37:30.020
And actually in Johnson
their first witness

00:37:30.020 --> 00:37:32.520
I dessimated and in Pilliad

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:33.860
the first witness I dessimated.

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:35.460
It clearly worked.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:36.840
- So in those two cases,

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:39.020
who did Monsanto put up first?

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:42.550
Was it the same person and
what were the strategies

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:44.910
and tactics that you used to

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:47.080
take down their first witness?

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:48.080
- Well' it's kind of weird

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:49.690
because every time I cross examine

00:37:49.690 --> 00:37:52.523
one of Monsanto's witnesses
we never see them again.

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:55.080
I think they just quit.

00:37:55.080 --> 00:37:56.110
They go I'm not doing this anymore.

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.690
This is not worth it.

00:37:57.690 --> 00:37:59.640
So in the Johnson case,

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:01.840
their first witness was
this guy named Al-Khatib,

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.760
He was a weeds scientist,

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:05.530
and it was hilarious.

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:07.482
- Weeds as in like the things
that grow in the yards.

00:38:07.482 --> 00:38:08.315
- Yeah, exactly.

00:38:08.315 --> 00:38:09.148
He wasn't like a weed scientist.

00:38:09.148 --> 00:38:10.327
- I'm in San Francisco so I had to ask.

00:38:10.327 --> 00:38:11.160
- No, I mean.

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:12.070
That would've been funny.

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:13.930
But no he's actually how to kill weeds.

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:14.763
- Okay.

00:38:14.763 --> 00:38:16.880
People who smoke weed don't like this guy.

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:17.893
Cause he kills weed.

00:38:18.996 --> 00:38:21.383
In any event, he was up there and

00:38:21.383 --> 00:38:22.620
it was really funny.

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:24.400
He was wearing a suit with a purple tie.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:25.993
And it was this whole get up.

00:38:27.111 --> 00:38:28.700
And when I was cross examining him I go

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:31.170
isn't it true that you told the world

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:33.450
that this international
agency is actually very

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:35.090
respected and we have
to consider what they

00:38:35.090 --> 00:38:37.470
say when they say something causes cancer.

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:38.490
He's like I've never said that.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:40.180
I go really well, here is a news article.

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:41.720
I pulled this up out of Google

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:43.307
where he literally was saying that.

00:38:43.307 --> 00:38:45.450
And he's like I never
said that at any meeting

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:46.440
I go oh really?

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.190
I handed him the article.

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:49.023
He looks at it.

00:38:49.023 --> 00:38:49.947
And he goes.

00:38:49.947 --> 00:38:53.110
- I go, you see that picture, yeah,

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:54.523
that's you right?

00:38:55.650 --> 00:38:56.820
Yeah.

00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:57.900
See that tie right there?

00:38:57.900 --> 00:38:58.733
Yeah.

00:38:58.733 --> 00:38:59.980
That's the same tie
you're wearing right now.

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:00.813
Isn't it?

00:39:00.813 --> 00:39:01.646
- No, no.

00:39:01.646 --> 00:39:02.479
- It was.

00:39:02.479 --> 00:39:03.590
- Experts buy more than one tie

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:04.540
before you testify.

00:39:05.502 --> 00:39:06.335
- And he was just like

00:39:06.335 --> 00:39:07.279
this is my favorite tie.

00:39:07.279 --> 00:39:08.750
And I'm like, oh it looks good on you sir.

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:10.230
But you did say that didn't you?

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:12.010
He was like, yeah.

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:12.843
And then at one point I was,

00:39:12.843 --> 00:39:15.230
you have a financial
interest in whether or not

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:16.063
people spray weeds.

00:39:16.063 --> 00:39:16.896
He's like no I don't,

00:39:16.896 --> 00:39:19.220
I go, don't you have patent?

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:20.053
I do?

00:39:20.053 --> 00:39:21.290
Yeah, I handed him a U.S. patent,

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:23.260
under oath with his name I mean.

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:24.460
I dessimated the guy.

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:25.750
And then in the Johnson case

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:27.920
their first witness was Doctor Bellow.

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:29.420
And we had a moment,

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:32.340
between when she first starts testifying

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:33.950
and before she offers an opinion.

00:39:33.950 --> 00:39:36.220
They do something, they
lay your foundations.

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:37.390
Are you an expert?

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:39.040
Can you testify about things?

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:41.530
And then there is a
short period of Voir dire

00:39:41.530 --> 00:39:43.680
and it's funny because in mock trial

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:46.090
we always use that opportunity to attack

00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:47.270
the witness's credibility.

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:48.120
- Right.

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:49.870
- But during our trial
Monsanto didn't do that

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:52.273
with a single one of our experts.

00:39:52.273 --> 00:39:53.106
- Ha.

00:39:53.106 --> 00:39:53.939
- They'd go, the judge would go,

00:39:53.939 --> 00:39:55.590
would you like to Voir dire,
and I'd say no your honor

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:56.600
we rest on our papers.

00:39:56.600 --> 00:39:58.510
Okay and then we'd proceed.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:00.340
Here, the first one is would you voir dire

00:40:00.340 --> 00:40:01.640
and I'd go yes your honor.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:03.240
I would like to voir dire.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:04.330
And I walked to the defense counsel

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:05.197
and I go you should sit down.

00:40:05.197 --> 00:40:06.790
This is gonna take a while.

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:08.750
And I just took her apart.

00:40:08.750 --> 00:40:10.380
- So the voir dire process

00:40:10.380 --> 00:40:11.700
when it comes to an expert is

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:14.860
to test their credentials
to make sure that

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:16.260
they are capable of testifying.

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.529
For some reason we use
the same french term.

00:40:18.529 --> 00:40:19.362
- Yeah.

00:40:19.362 --> 00:40:20.480
- That means to speak and to say,

00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:23.250
for both jury selection and for experts.

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:26.340
I don't know why, but, so.

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:29.380
That's pretty unusual because
when it comes to these

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:32.620
multi million dollar pieces of litigation

00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:35.350
there is your daubert
motion and you've already

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:37.360
tested a lot of the gatekeeping functions

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.240
of the judge but,

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:41.490
you still have an opportunity
to kind of put your

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:44.220
chips on the table or try
and take their chips away

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:46.050
from their expert opinion.

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:46.883
- That's right.

00:40:46.883 --> 00:40:50.090
- So, it's not surprising
to me that most lawyers

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:52.550
and probably most defense
layers are not going

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:54.877
to be in the habit of
really taking advantage of

00:40:54.877 --> 00:40:57.200
voir dire but again, it's a great example

00:40:57.200 --> 00:41:00.660
of having an opportunity
and taking it in a way

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:02.610
that I think most lawyers
are not going to do.

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:04.830
- Yeah, and it also depends on the expert.

00:41:04.830 --> 00:41:05.663
I mean if they're really truly

00:41:05.663 --> 00:41:08.290
the most spectacularly qualified

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:10.480
it's not worth your time.

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:12.103
Like to give you some context.

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:14.721
Juxtapositions.

00:41:14.721 --> 00:41:15.554
- Sure.

00:41:15.554 --> 00:41:18.180
- The first expert I
go through her resume,

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:20.060
and her CV, I talk about her publications,

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:21.820
and basically at the end of it,

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:23.920
I kind of hammer her pretty
hard at the end of it,

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:26.790
she goes yeah I guess really
determining what causes cancer

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:27.750
isn't my expertise.

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:29.370
That's what she testified.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.020
At that moment I stop,

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.621
I look at the jury,

00:41:32.621 --> 00:41:33.454
don't look at the judge,

00:41:33.454 --> 00:41:34.287
I go, your honor,

00:41:34.287 --> 00:41:35.320
I'm looking at the jury,

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:37.630
at this time I move to exclude
this witness's testimony

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:38.780
in its entirety.

00:41:38.780 --> 00:41:41.093
She just admitted she's
not even an expert.

00:41:42.010 --> 00:41:43.160
And I'm not even looking at the judge,

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:43.993
I'm looking at the jury,

00:41:43.993 --> 00:41:46.000
the jury is like smiling and laughing.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:46.833
Kind of giggling.

00:41:46.833 --> 00:41:48.290
Because they know I just took her apart.

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:50.497
And the judge goes, denied Mr,. Wisner.

00:41:50.497 --> 00:41:51.770
You may proceed.

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:53.410
So I sit down and so the first time he

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:55.858
testifies about an actual opinion,

00:41:55.858 --> 00:41:57.320
she just admitted she's not an expert

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:58.420
and the jury just thinks she's a joke.

00:41:58.420 --> 00:41:59.960
So that was the first witness.

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.530
- The damage is done
regardless of whether the judge

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:05.160
has excluded her testimony.

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:05.993
- Exactly.

00:42:05.993 --> 00:42:07.450
- You've done the rhetorical damage

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:10.430
whether or not is admitted
into evidence or not.

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:11.520
- Precisely.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:13.180
- But then the next witness

00:42:13.180 --> 00:42:14.760
was actually my witness again.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:17.070
And he had a PhD from UCLA whatever

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:19.340
and actually his opinions
weren't really that important

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:21.110
but I figured whatever,

00:42:21.110 --> 00:42:22.560
so the judge goes, Mr.
Wisner would you like

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:25.000
to voir dire and again
I go, yes your honor.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:26.953
And the jury is like oh
what's he gonna do this time.

00:42:26.953 --> 00:42:29.280
This is gonna be really intense.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:31.410
Cause last one was so brutal.

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:32.750
I go up there and I go,

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:35.620
Doctor Filling, I have a very important

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:37.200
question from you

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:38.910
Yeah, and I go, isn't UCLA

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:40.607
the greatest University in the World?

00:42:40.607 --> 00:42:41.553
And he goes,

00:42:42.903 --> 00:42:43.910
yeah.

00:42:43.910 --> 00:42:45.580
Good, I went there for college.

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:46.705
No questions your honor.

00:42:46.705 --> 00:42:48.080
I sat down.

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:49.730
And the jury is laughing.

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:50.590
The judge is laughing.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:51.444
Everyone is laughing.

00:42:51.444 --> 00:42:55.110
I've underscored how bad
the first witness was

00:42:55.110 --> 00:42:56.070
and then at the same time

00:42:56.070 --> 00:42:58.270
I've endeared myself to the court.

00:42:58.270 --> 00:42:59.103
- That's awesome.

00:42:59.103 --> 00:43:02.430
And also, it's good to get
that in as matter of law

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:05.720
that UCLA is the best
university out there.

00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:07.320
Don't think it can be disrupted.

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:11.290
- Often these experts are going to be

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:13.630
really world class experts in their field.

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:15.510
They're going to be talking about

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:19.499
some kind of medical thing,

00:43:19.499 --> 00:43:20.860
or mathematical thing,

00:43:20.860 --> 00:43:22.780
or some science based thing that

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:25.690
we often don't have
that kind of background.

00:43:25.690 --> 00:43:28.530
How do you go about preparing for

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:31.470
cross examining some other size witness

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:34.550
who has probably gone to
school and specialized in

00:43:35.433 --> 00:43:38.780
some part of science
that we have heard about

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:39.870
for the purposes of this trial

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:41.510
but we don't have that kind of background

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:43.230
how do you go toe to toe with someone

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:45.940
who is an expert in their filed?

00:43:45.940 --> 00:43:47.540
- During the Johnson case,

00:43:47.540 --> 00:43:50.640
they called this very well
respected toxicologist.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:52.670
Warren Foster to the stand.

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:54.000
And he was from Canada.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:56.473
And he had this long gray ponytail.

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:00.740
He's walking up to the
stand to get ready for

00:44:00.740 --> 00:44:01.820
his cross examination.

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:03.710
The jury hasn't come in yet.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:04.760
I walk up to the stand and I go

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:06.510
hey doctor can I ask you a quick question?

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.343
Personal question.

00:44:07.343 --> 00:44:08.480
He's like, yeah what's up?

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:10.083
And I go, the pony tail?

00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:12.210
Are you a hippie?

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:14.080
Or do you ride Harley's?

00:44:14.080 --> 00:44:15.330
And he looks at me and he goes.

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.793
I don't ride motorcycles.

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:19.073
He's a hippie.

00:44:19.073 --> 00:44:20.390
And made me like the guy.

00:44:20.390 --> 00:44:24.360
And so my actual cross examination
of him was very pleasant.

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:25.310
I was smiling.

00:44:25.310 --> 00:44:26.143
I was laughing.

00:44:26.143 --> 00:44:27.040
I was giggling.

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:27.873
I wasn't mean.

00:44:27.873 --> 00:44:30.513
I was very very sort of jovial.

00:44:31.870 --> 00:44:34.270
And I got him to say things that he never

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:35.610
in a million years would have said.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:38.090
His big argument was that
these tumors and these

00:44:38.090 --> 00:44:41.720
rodent studies, fell
within the historical range

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:43.560
which is the percentage of mice

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:44.740
that would get that tumor naturally.

00:44:44.740 --> 00:44:45.773
The background rate.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:49.080
- So they've been dosed with,

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:50.260
roundup probably.

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:51.360
- Glyphosatebut but yeah.

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:52.193
- Glyphosate.

00:44:52.193 --> 00:44:54.480
So one of the active
ingredients in roundup,

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:56.980
these mice have been doused with this

00:44:56.980 --> 00:45:01.150
some of them statistically
are gonna get cancer tumors.

00:45:01.150 --> 00:45:02.830
- This just by virtue of happenstance.

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:03.663
- Right.

00:45:03.663 --> 00:45:05.580
- And so if you have
elevated rates of tumors

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:07.571
it has to be above that
background rate or else

00:45:07.571 --> 00:45:09.940
you don't know if it's
just the background rate.

00:45:09.940 --> 00:45:12.300
And his whole arguments
was that they fell within

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:13.200
that background rate.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:16.070
So what that rate was
was the issue, right?

00:45:16.070 --> 00:45:19.720
He was saying it's 12%, my
experts were saying it's 2%.

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:21.350
And which one is it?

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:24.730
So he goes up there, and I go well doctor,

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:26.720
you'd agree with me sir,

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:30.190
numbers they're important, right?

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:32.260
And he goes, damn straight they are.

00:45:32.260 --> 00:45:34.970
Especially when it's the
number on my paycheck.

00:45:34.970 --> 00:45:38.590
And he didn't understand
what he was doing.

00:45:38.590 --> 00:45:42.420
He thought he was being
an everyman person.

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:43.260
Relating to everyone.

00:45:43.260 --> 00:45:45.450
Cause everyone cares about
the number on their paycheck.

00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:47.340
But it really looked like he was saying

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:49.020
make sure Monsanto pays me all the dollars

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:51.480
they have to pay me to
give this testimony.

00:45:51.480 --> 00:45:53.380
And then I go through and it was amazing.

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:54.800
It was a very Perry Mason moment.

00:45:54.800 --> 00:45:56.530
Very dramatic.

00:45:56.530 --> 00:45:57.980
He's talking about the
background rate and he says,

00:45:57.980 --> 00:45:59.140
it's 12% it's 12%.

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:00.880
So you're saying the average is 12%.

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:02.090
He goes yes.

00:46:02.090 --> 00:46:03.580
I go all right, let's look at it.

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:05.400
So I put up the study that he said,

00:46:05.400 --> 00:46:06.730
and I go through all the numbers.

00:46:06.730 --> 00:46:08.090
And it's 1% percent it's 2% it's 1%,

00:46:08.090 --> 00:46:10.487
it's 1%, 1%, and there is like

00:46:10.487 --> 00:46:12.540
40 numbers and they're all one or two.

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:15.647
There is one of them that has 12%.

00:46:15.647 --> 00:46:17.370
And I go sir when you
said it was the average,

00:46:17.370 --> 00:46:20.100
I go I'm not the best at math,

00:46:20.100 --> 00:46:25.100
but look at how in the world
does that average to 12%?

00:46:25.100 --> 00:46:27.750
And he goes, picking up his glasses.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:28.850
He's reading it closer.

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:30.483
There's like a long pause and he goes.

00:46:32.718 --> 00:46:34.143
Uh, yeah.

00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:36.313
Guess I'm just wrong.

00:46:37.350 --> 00:46:39.200
I go, doctor.

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:40.610
It's a big man to admit it.

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:41.467
No further questions.

00:46:41.467 --> 00:46:42.850
And I sat down.

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:44.550
That was the last
question we asked the guy.

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:46.250
And that was the last question they asked

00:46:46.250 --> 00:46:47.170
and they couldn't even rebuild him.

00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:49.275
It was like get him off the stand.

00:46:49.275 --> 00:46:50.260
That's how we kicked their butt.

00:46:50.260 --> 00:46:51.093
- Yeah, well.

00:46:51.093 --> 00:46:53.756
That's why you get tagged with
a $2 billion dollar verdict.

00:46:53.756 --> 00:46:54.589
- Yeah.

00:46:54.589 --> 00:46:57.100
- Alright, so inevitably
when you get one of these

00:46:57.100 --> 00:47:00.530
multi million dollar or
multi billion dollar verdicts

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:02.490
you get these people who say,

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:04.480
this is just a greedy plaintiff

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:06.680
or a greedy plaintiff's attorney

00:47:06.680 --> 00:47:09.710
who's just looking for a payday.

00:47:09.710 --> 00:47:10.800
The science isn't there.

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:12.600
I've used roundup my whole life.

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:15.210
I've never contracted anything.

00:47:15.210 --> 00:47:17.880
What do you say to those people,

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.080
especially since potentially they could be

00:47:20.080 --> 00:47:22.315
on your next jury?

00:47:22.315 --> 00:47:23.540
- I mean I have to say that

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:25.587
the strongest piece of
evidence in our case

00:47:25.587 --> 00:47:26.980
was actually the science.

00:47:26.980 --> 00:47:29.550
Which is, I kind of
talked about this earlier.

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:31.414
It's why we won.

00:47:31.414 --> 00:47:33.330
Scientistic on our jury,
if you can get them.

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:35.430
Because they understand it.

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:36.700
Look at the science of
roundup and Glyphosate.

00:47:36.700 --> 00:47:39.183
There is really three pillars.

00:47:40.112 --> 00:47:42.010
There is studies that
look at actual humans

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:43.340
who've been exposed in the real world

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:44.930
and says okay, all the
people who are spraying

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:46.960
roundup do they get more cancer?

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:48.690
That's the epidemiology.

00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:50.530
Then there is the sort
of middle pillar which is

00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:53.500
the sort of cellular
studies and that's like okay

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:55.670
when we apply roundup to a human cell,

00:47:55.670 --> 00:47:57.240
what happens to the cell?

00:47:57.240 --> 00:47:59.560
And specifically, we're looking
for does it cause genetic

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:01.797
damage because that's how
you cause the mutation

00:48:01.797 --> 00:48:03.850
that leads to cancer.

00:48:03.850 --> 00:48:06.640
And then the third pillar
is the animal studies.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.390
They have these laboratory
experiments where

00:48:08.390 --> 00:48:11.370
they study these animals
for their entire lifetimes

00:48:11.370 --> 00:48:13.570
and it's highly controlled and they see,

00:48:13.570 --> 00:48:15.700
here is some that aren't
exposed to any Glyphosate,

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:17.140
here are some that are,

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:19.060
and are the ones that are
being exposed to Glyphosate

00:48:19.060 --> 00:48:20.389
do they get more tumors?

00:48:20.389 --> 00:48:21.222
- Right.

00:48:21.222 --> 00:48:23.150
- And when you put all
the science together

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:24.920
it's pretty overwhelming.

00:48:24.920 --> 00:48:26.280
For example, in the animal studies,

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:27.893
and every single mouse
study that's been done,

00:48:27.893 --> 00:48:30.650
I think there's been six or seven of them,

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:31.880
every single one

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:34.420
there is elevated rates
of malignant lymphoma,

00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:36.090
and we're talking about lymphoma.

00:48:36.090 --> 00:48:37.510
You look at the cellular studies,

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:40.560
it shows genetic damage in
human lymphocytes which is

00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:42.200
how yo get lymphoma.

00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:43.790
And when you look at the human data

00:48:43.790 --> 00:48:45.668
it shows that people who
are generally exposed to

00:48:45.668 --> 00:48:49.200
roundup have greater rates of lymphoma.

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:52.960
Across the board it's pointing
in a specific direction now.

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:55.210
One of the things that's really important

00:48:55.210 --> 00:48:58.240
and you spend a lot of time
educating the jury about this

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:01.130
in voir dire and in your opening statement

00:49:01.130 --> 00:49:02.770
is the burden of proof.

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:05.270
Cause if I had to prove something

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:07.380
with absolute certainty
that it causes cancer

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:08.950
it would be almost impossible.

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:11.070
I mean that is a high high burden.

00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:13.709
But we just have to do
more likely than not.

00:49:13.709 --> 00:49:15.660
- The preponderance of the evidence.

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:16.630
- Exactly.

00:49:16.630 --> 00:49:20.087
We always thought, you have
a feather weight scale right?

00:49:20.087 --> 00:49:23.210
And if one feather on one
side just slightly moves

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:24.123
then we win.

00:49:24.123 --> 00:49:26.037
They way I like to tell a jury is,

00:49:26.037 --> 00:49:28.793
if you hear all the
evidence and you go well,

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:33.050
I think it causes
cancer, but I'm not sure.

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:34.300
Good, we win.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:35.780
That's all we need to do.

00:49:35.780 --> 00:49:37.260
I think it might cause cancer.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:38.310
That's it.

00:49:38.310 --> 00:49:40.100
And when you have that lower standard

00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:41.980
which is very different
than the criminal context

00:49:41.980 --> 00:49:43.960
but in the civil context,

00:49:43.960 --> 00:49:45.660
it's not that hard to prove.

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:47.650
Now if we had to prove
with absolutely certainty

00:49:47.650 --> 00:49:49.360
I think that would be a
much different question.

00:49:49.360 --> 00:49:51.840
And I think very often people will go

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:54.100
you're just making up signs or
you're not actually doing it

00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:55.200
or this is just plaintiff's lawyers

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:57.800
inflaming the jury's emotions.

00:49:57.800 --> 00:49:59.870
They don't understand the
legal system and actually

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:01.680
the juries are properly applying

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:03.100
and weighing the evidence in this case.

00:50:03.100 --> 00:50:05.540
- Yeah that's the big difference
between the civil system

00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:09.910
that is built to compensate
victims for their damages

00:50:09.910 --> 00:50:13.430
versus the criminal system where the state

00:50:13.430 --> 00:50:16.990
has to prove a crime
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:50:16.990 --> 00:50:18.509
Which is a very very high standard.

00:50:18.509 --> 00:50:19.400
- That's right.

00:50:19.400 --> 00:50:22.550
- You can't ever numerically compare

00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:24.640
reasonable doubt to
preponderance of the evidence

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:28.470
but if it's 51% versus
49% in terms of certainty

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:29.890
then the plaintiff should win if

00:50:29.890 --> 00:50:31.940
they prove 51%.

00:50:31.940 --> 00:50:35.000
And unfortunately I think a lot of people

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:36.869
don't understand the difference
between the burden of proof

00:50:36.869 --> 00:50:39.190
in criminal law versus civil law.

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:41.910
- And that's why we spend so much time

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:44.800
educating the jury about that exact issue

00:50:45.694 --> 00:50:48.510
from day one, from voir
dire, or picking the jury.

00:50:48.510 --> 00:50:49.700
The other thing that's really complicated

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:52.250
is there is actually a third burden.

00:50:52.250 --> 00:50:54.190
And that's for punitive damages right?

00:50:54.190 --> 00:50:57.370
You have to show that they
acted with deliberate disregard

00:50:57.370 --> 00:50:58.630
to human safety.

00:50:58.630 --> 00:51:02.500
You have to show, that
they acted with malice,

00:51:02.500 --> 00:51:04.290
and you have to show
that not by preponderance

00:51:04.290 --> 00:51:07.350
of the evidence but by clear
and convincing evidence,

00:51:07.350 --> 00:51:10.030
which is somewhere in the sort of scale.

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:11.104
- Yeah somewhere between the two.

00:51:11.104 --> 00:51:11.937
- Exactly.

00:51:11.937 --> 00:51:13.850
- Between preponderance and
beyond reasonable doubt,

00:51:13.850 --> 00:51:16.150
we don't know where but it's
somewhere in between it.

00:51:16.150 --> 00:51:17.510
And I think for that one,

00:51:17.510 --> 00:51:20.880
you simply say, does
anyone here really believe

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:23.890
that Monsanto wasn't trying
to make money knowing that

00:51:23.890 --> 00:51:24.723
it causes cancer?

00:51:24.723 --> 00:51:25.590
You know what yeah.

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:26.546
Then we're done.

00:51:26.546 --> 00:51:30.920
Getting to the malice proof
is actually relatively easy.

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:32.856
In all three juries
that has been unanimous

00:51:32.856 --> 00:51:33.689
in each case.

00:51:33.689 --> 00:51:35.630
- Yeah, wow that's incredible.

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:37.410
It's also important to
educate the jury because

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:40.830
the term malice in the
punitive damages standard

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:42.850
is not what we would normally think of

00:51:42.850 --> 00:51:43.683
in terms of malice.

00:51:43.683 --> 00:51:44.830
It's not like someone at Monsanto

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:45.843
was like I hate these people

00:51:45.843 --> 00:51:48.110
and I'm going to make them suffer.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:50.310
It's a term of art that has been defined

00:51:50.310 --> 00:51:53.010
by hundreds of pages of case law,

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:55.783
and jury instructions and statutory law.

00:51:57.140 --> 00:51:58.900
So, you have to educate
the jury and sometimes

00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:00.150
you have to educate the judge

00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:02.800
at every step of the way.

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:05.710
Because we're tried by a jury of our peers

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:08.720
who are people that are not lawyers

00:52:08.720 --> 00:52:11.060
and they're not steeped in this every day

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:11.893
like we are.

00:52:11.893 --> 00:52:14.670
So, the education point is huge.

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:16.660
And I think you know it's something that

00:52:16.660 --> 00:52:18.920
when people get angry
about this kind of verdict

00:52:18.920 --> 00:52:22.540
people say oh, $2 billion
dollars, that's outrageous.

00:52:22.540 --> 00:52:24.640
This is what's wrong with the jury system.

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:28.610
Those people don't understand
just how important the

00:52:28.610 --> 00:52:32.170
jury system is to the way we practice law.

00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:34.350
Because believe it or not,

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:37.190
juries very often get it right.

00:52:37.190 --> 00:52:38.730
Sometimes they make mistakes, for sure,

00:52:38.730 --> 00:52:40.770
but they're more often right than wrong.

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:43.280
And it's a method that's fair,

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:44.850
and it's transparent,

00:52:44.850 --> 00:52:47.580
and it gives us confidence
that things are being

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:49.130
adjudicated by our peers.

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:51.387
And people who want to get away with,

00:52:51.387 --> 00:52:52.840
disregard the jury system,

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:54.170
they're either defense lawyers,

00:52:54.170 --> 00:52:55.690
sorry Mr. Stone.

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:58.060
- I've spent a lot of time
as a plaintiff's lawyer.

00:52:58.060 --> 00:52:58.893
- Okay.

00:52:58.893 --> 00:53:02.240
Either were defense lawyers
or they've never been

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:04.430
in a situation where they
felt they needed recourse

00:53:04.430 --> 00:53:05.497
to the jury system
because if you're someone

00:53:05.497 --> 00:53:08.320
who is hurt, if you're
someone who's been harmed

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:10.960
or really want to make a
change because you see like

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:12.550
something is wrong in the world,

00:53:12.550 --> 00:53:15.660
taking your access to the jury system away

00:53:15.660 --> 00:53:17.630
is a real problem.

00:53:17.630 --> 00:53:18.463
- Yeah.

00:53:18.463 --> 00:53:19.880
- And I think that very often with

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:21.870
tort reformers or the hot coffee people,

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:24.490
and these people who think
oh these juries are crazy.

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:25.840
When you really dig down deep

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:28.070
you'll find out that, like
for example the hot coffee

00:53:28.070 --> 00:53:29.010
story is how we think.

00:53:29.010 --> 00:53:31.690
There is a great documentary about it.

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:32.593
And I think it's really
important for people

00:53:32.593 --> 00:53:35.800
to take a step back and not
listen to that nonsense.

00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:37.700
And actually say, okay if I was hurt,

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:38.670
what would I want?

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:40.680
And if you were hurt you'd
want to have a jury too.

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:41.600
- Yeah, so wait.

00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:45.850
So you're suggesting that Twitter
law degree is not the same

00:53:45.850 --> 00:53:49.420
as being a member of a
jury and watching a trial

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:53.010
from start to finish for nine weeks

00:53:53.010 --> 00:53:55.390
and learning everything
from start to finish.

00:53:55.390 --> 00:53:56.464
I find that hard to believe.

00:53:56.464 --> 00:53:57.390
- Shocking.

00:53:57.390 --> 00:53:58.223
- Yeah.

00:53:58.223 --> 00:53:59.056
- Who would've ever though?

00:53:59.056 --> 00:53:59.889
- It's crazy.

00:53:59.889 --> 00:54:00.722
- Yeah.

00:54:00.722 --> 00:54:02.390
- Just reading one article
or maybe a Tweet storm

00:54:02.390 --> 00:54:04.590
about it is not the same as actually being

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:07.790
on that jury and listening
to the facts on both sides

00:54:07.790 --> 00:54:08.836
for weeks at a time.

00:54:08.836 --> 00:54:09.870
- I'll tell you what.

00:54:09.870 --> 00:54:11.820
You take those people who are the trolls

00:54:11.820 --> 00:54:14.221
and the people who think they know better,

00:54:14.221 --> 00:54:16.590
you put them on one of my juries,

00:54:16.590 --> 00:54:18.950
in the seven weeks, they'll
be singing the same tune.

00:54:18.950 --> 00:54:22.760
Because unless they're
dishonest with themselves

00:54:22.760 --> 00:54:24.420
the evidence in this case is overwhelming

00:54:24.420 --> 00:54:25.770
and there is a reason why the juries

00:54:25.770 --> 00:54:28.280
have been unanimous so consistently.

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:30.110
I mean these are people from
different walks of life,

00:54:30.110 --> 00:54:31.720
all over the state of California

00:54:31.720 --> 00:54:33.460
who keep coming to the same conclusion

00:54:33.460 --> 00:54:34.880
when they see the evidence.

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.330
It's because it causes cancer
and it's because Monsanto knew

00:54:37.330 --> 00:54:40.600
it causes cancer and
they just didn't care.

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:42.780
- So, in the Johnson case,

00:54:42.780 --> 00:54:46.110
you originally won $289 million dollars

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:47.363
on behalf of the plaintiff.

00:54:47.363 --> 00:54:48.540
- That's right.

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:53.210
- That was later chopped down
by the judge in briefing.

00:54:53.210 --> 00:54:56.650
But my understanding is that the jury

00:54:56.650 --> 00:55:00.750
on their own time wanted
to dispute with the judge

00:55:00.750 --> 00:55:03.590
chopping their own verdict down.

00:55:03.590 --> 00:55:05.780
Can you talk about what happened there?

00:55:05.780 --> 00:55:06.690
First of all is that true?

00:55:06.690 --> 00:55:09.360
Did the jury come back to support you?

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:11.190
- It's unbelievable.

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:15.050
And we had no role in trying
to get the jury there okay?

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:18.200
So after the verdict, the
judge has post trial motions

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:21.470
where she hears arguments
as to why maybe the verdict

00:55:21.470 --> 00:55:23.660
should be thrown out or the verdict

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:25.247
should be reduced or whatever.

00:55:25.247 --> 00:55:26.990
And she had written a tentative ruling

00:55:26.990 --> 00:55:29.390
which is her initial thoughts

00:55:29.390 --> 00:55:30.920
of what she's thinking about doing

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:33.900
and in her tentative she
had actually gotten rid

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:36.600
of the punitives and said,
we have to have a new trial

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:41.020
and she had basically said
I'm very seriously considering

00:55:41.020 --> 00:55:42.290
getting rid of the rest of the case

00:55:42.290 --> 00:55:44.840
basically trowing out the whole verdict.

00:55:44.840 --> 00:55:47.510
- And that's crazy because
judges rarely deviate

00:55:47.510 --> 00:55:48.430
from their tentative ruling.

00:55:48.430 --> 00:55:49.840
If they went to the trouble

00:55:49.840 --> 00:55:52.530
of actually writing down what
they think they want to do

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:54.650
the odds of you being
able to convince them to

00:55:54.650 --> 00:55:57.070
go against that and here,
she was saying to get rid

00:55:57.070 --> 00:55:58.600
of all the punitive damages

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:01.000
which is most of the $289 million-

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:01.833
- That's right.

00:56:01.833 --> 00:56:03.950
- I mean the odds of being
able to talk judges down

00:56:03.950 --> 00:56:06.820
from that are maybe one in 10.

00:56:06.820 --> 00:56:08.840
- Yeah, probably less.

00:56:08.840 --> 00:56:09.830
So we get there

00:56:11.499 --> 00:56:15.140
and there is 10 of the jurors are there

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:16.630
to hear the argument.

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:18.120
They've read the tentative.

00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:20.600
And they're furious about
what the judge is doing.

00:56:20.600 --> 00:56:22.170
- This is how long after trial?

00:56:22.170 --> 00:56:24.140
- This is about six weeks.

00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:25.840
Almost six or seven weeks after the trial.

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:30.270
- So 10 of the 12 jurors
came back a month and a half

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:33.150
later after their
service was already done.

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:33.983
- That's right.

00:56:33.983 --> 00:56:35.310
They have no reason to be there.

00:56:35.310 --> 00:56:37.100
They can just go on
living their marry lives.

00:56:37.100 --> 00:56:38.240
They've done their job.

00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:40.040
But they care so much about this case.

00:56:40.040 --> 00:56:41.670
They care so much about their verdict.

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:43.750
They spent so much of
their life doing this,

00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:45.240
they want to see what happens.

00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:46.440
And so they all show up,

00:56:47.435 --> 00:56:50.403
and we have this argument
and it's a rough argument.

00:56:51.350 --> 00:56:54.833
Monsanto is accusing me of
all sorts of wrongdoing.

00:56:55.720 --> 00:56:56.760
And it goes back and forth.

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:58.450
And that was on a Friday.

00:56:58.450 --> 00:57:01.900
That weekend those jurors go on a

00:57:01.900 --> 00:57:04.682
it's amazing they go on Twitter,

00:57:04.682 --> 00:57:06.840
they start talking to news reporters,

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:08.740
they go on the local television.

00:57:08.740 --> 00:57:10.700
They write letters to
the judge telling her

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:11.830
why she shouldn't do this.

00:57:11.830 --> 00:57:13.270
Why it's wrong.

00:57:13.270 --> 00:57:14.103
And

00:57:15.830 --> 00:57:17.750
the judge turned around her tentative.

00:57:17.750 --> 00:57:19.420
She kept the verdict.

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:21.747
And she did reduce the
punitive damages down.

00:57:21.747 --> 00:57:23.577
It was a $289 million verdict.

00:57:23.577 --> 00:57:25.940
She reduced the whole
thing to about 80 million.

00:57:25.940 --> 00:57:27.600
So there was a big reduction

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:29.360
but she gave something
called a remittitur.

00:57:29.360 --> 00:57:30.460
Which is sort of interesting.

00:57:30.460 --> 00:57:31.970
It's a complicated legal issue.

00:57:31.970 --> 00:57:35.710
Basically, the judge is reducing
it and if Monsanto pays it,

00:57:35.710 --> 00:57:37.440
the case is done.

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:40.260
But if Monsanto appeals it,

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:43.040
then we have the right
to appeal the reduction.

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:44.580
So if we accept the remittitur

00:57:44.580 --> 00:57:46.610
we cannot appeal it after they pay it,

00:57:46.610 --> 00:57:47.450
but if.

00:57:47.450 --> 00:57:48.760
So it was sort of a trade off.

00:57:48.760 --> 00:57:50.830
And Monsanto obviously
wants to roll the dice

00:57:50.830 --> 00:57:53.007
they want to play a $200 million

00:57:53.007 --> 00:57:55.970
game of chicken on the appeal.

00:57:55.970 --> 00:57:56.830
And we feel pretty confident

00:57:56.830 --> 00:57:57.663
that were actually gonna get

00:57:57.663 --> 00:57:59.390
the money back so we'll see how it goes.

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:03.570
But this jury cared so
much about this case.

00:58:03.570 --> 00:58:04.840
I've never seen a jury do that.

00:58:04.840 --> 00:58:06.070
It's just unheard of.

00:58:06.070 --> 00:58:07.810
And actually one of the jurors

00:58:07.810 --> 00:58:11.230
